From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 06:06:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: per-session chat features, starting with Run suggested
 commands

Some chat features need both a web renderer and an instruction telling the
agent to write in the form the renderer expects. The Run button is the
first: most agent code blocks carry no language tag, so it only finds shell
commands reliably when the agent is asked to tag them as bash. That
instruction costs prompt tokens on every session, and not every user or
agent wants the convention, so each feature is now a per-session toggle
that drops both its instruction and its UI.

- omnigent/runtime/chat_features.py holds the feature list: a key, a
  title and description for the UI, and the instruction text. Adding a
  feature is one entry plus its renderer. GET /v1/chat-features serves the
  titles and descriptions to the web.
- Each session stores its enabled set in session_overrides (no migration),
  resolved at create from the request, then the creator's saved default,
  then every feature. Forks copy the parent's set, sub-agent and mirrored
  child sessions inherit it, scheduled and imported sessions use the
  owner's default. Sessions from before this setting get every feature.
  PATCH /v1/sessions/{id} takes chat_features; null resets it to the
  caller's default.
- The per-user default is a new shared-preference key,
  {"kind": "chat_features", "name": "default"}: an array of feature keys,
  stored sorted and unique, where [] enables none and null removes the
  choice (every feature). Keys a build doesn't know are kept in storage and
  ignored when a session resolves them, so builds that add features can
  share the value. Session create reads it server-side with the same
  user/workspace identity as the shared routes; a failed read falls back to
  every feature instead of failing the create.
- The default needs shared preferences enabled on the server
  (shared_preferences_authority_id in the omnigent server config; the
  Docker and Databricks entry points don't wire it upstream, and embedders
  pass a preference store to create_app). Without it, new sessions get
  every feature, per-session toggles still work, and Settings > General
  shows the default switches disabled with a note.
- The web reads and saves the default through GET /v1/preferences/shared
  and POST /v1/preferences/shared/operations: it follows pagination
  (restarting when an edit lands between pages), sends expected_version,
  retries an unanswered edit with the same operation_id, and on a conflict
  re-reads and retries once. Settings shows competing values from other
  devices with a Keep button each; saving resolves them. The installation
  ID is stable per browser.
- The runner receives the set in the session init snapshot and with every
  forwarded turn. SDK harnesses compose instructions per turn, so a change
  applies on the next turn. Native Claude and Codex take instructions only
  at launch (including omnigent claude/codex resumes, which now read the
  session's set), so the web menu notes that a change reaches the agent
  after the session restarts. Claude Code honours only the last
  --append-system-prompt, so ours is merged into a user-supplied one.
- The web adds the toggles to the new-chat dialog's agent menu, the session
  configure menu and Settings > General, and shows Run only when the
  session's set includes run_commands.

Signed-off-by: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
